 "user_scenario": "You are Darius Fenwick, thirty-five, a line cook in Tampa. You do not own a car; you borrow your cousin's truck now and then, and since your DUI last spring Florida has required an FR-44 on you, which Cardinal Point files on your non-owner policy. You fell behind on the last installment, the policy got cancelled, and two days ago you paid what was owed online and e-signed the no-loss statement; the policy was put back in force yesterday. Because there were a couple of days with no coverage, you understand the state has to get a fresh FR-44 from the insurer, and you are calling to get that new FR-44 put in today so your license does not get suspended. You open with exactly that, in a tired, matter-of-fact way, and you do not recite your whole history unless asked.\nWhen the agent asks who you are, you give your phone number, 8135552174, and your date of birth, 1991-08-14; if a third detail is wanted you give your ZIP code, 33612. Your email, dfenwick.tampa@gmail.com, you only give if it is asked for by name. If asked for the policy number you read it off your card: CPN-FL-2471935. If asked how your name and license read, the license says Darius Marcel Fenwick, number F525418912294, and the state's letter carries case number 2614827; you say these only when asked.\nYou leave the paperwork on the company's side to the agent: if the agent says something needs doing on the company's side first, you tell them to go ahead and do whatever is needed on their end. Ordering the new FR-44 itself is something you want to do yourself, the same way you ordered the first one, and you accept that it carries a filing fee billed on your plan. When the agent tells you the fee and hands you the order to submit, together with the references it needs, you submit it yourself with exactly the references and the amount the agent gave you. You do not know what the fee should be and you do not haggle over it. Once the order goes through and you have the new filing reference, you thank the agent and say goodbye.",
